unit: radiation-and-the-body

post blog-reading-a-scary-number
author: npc-mina
lesson: risk-in-perspective
title: Calm note: reading a scary number
hook: A frightening headline is only a number. Three questions make it something you can judge.
cover: headline
read: 1
unlock: {"trigger":"lesson_complete","lessonId":"risk-in-perspective"}
reactors: npc-ray
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

p:
    You read a headline about radiation, and your stomach drops. That is a normal reaction. Here is what I do next. I slow down and ask three questions.

h: Three questions

list:
    How big is the dose, in millisieverts?
    How fast did it arrive?
    How does it compare with doses I already know?

figure: headline
caption: A page with a headline, and a lens held over its number.
h: Two answers that are both wrong

p:
    One says any dose of radiation is deadly. The other says radiation is harmless. Neither is right.

quote:
    Size and speed decide what a dose does.

fact:
    A very large dose in a short time can make people ill, with symptoms such as nausea and vomiting within hours. Low levels do not cause immediate health effects, but they can add a small increase in the risk of cancer over a lifetime.

p:
    Cells are extremely efficient at repairing radiation damage. If a dose is low, or spread over a long time, the risk is lower because repair is more likely.

p:
    So next time, take a breath and ask the three questions. A number with a size, a speed and something to compare it with is much easier to hold.

next: try it on a headline => lesson risk-in-perspective

